WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.270
They're introducing

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:04.300
third world practices of
exploitation in the first world.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.560
Vote no on this corporate power grab.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.810
We're not getting benefits.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.440
And I knew that coming into this.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:10.610
I like the flexibility.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.830
Should Americans be allowed to freelance?

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.310
Progressive Democrats have
been pushing legislation

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:18.090
at the state and federal level
that would force companies

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.960
like Uber and Lyft in particular

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.280
to make all of their
independent contractors

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:23.890
full-time employees.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.300
Would you like to see
this California based law

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.600
go nationwide?

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:29.470
Well, of course.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.450
But that movement had a major setback

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.010
in the 2020 election
when California voters

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.080
came out in favor of severely limiting

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:38.170
the impact of a 2019 law

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:39.880
that, as it was originally written,

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.430
would have wiped out the
state's freelance economy.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.190
California voters appear to
have approved Proposition 22.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.630
Assembly Bill Five had
already been weakened

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:52.040
by several carve-outs, but a
referendum on the 2020 ballot

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.850
was the biggest blow to
the bill's original intent,

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.800
because it means that
Uber and Lyft drivers

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.340
will be mostly exempt
from its requirements.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.500
Back in May, Joe Biden called attempts

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.488
to gut AB5 unacceptable,

00:01:03.488 --> 00:01:06.800
urging Californians to
vote no on the referendum.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.640
Nevertheless, in this
overwhelmingly blue state,

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.840
almost 60% approved the
measure at the ballot box.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.510
When Reason covered AB5 earlier this year,

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.780
its most vocal opponents turned
out to be freelance writers

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.340
on both the left.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.500
This goes too far.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.333
And right.

00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:24.600
They're politicians,
they're union workers.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.790
There are people who don't get it.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:26.623
They're activists.

00:01:26.623 --> 00:01:28.290
They're cutting people
off from opportunity.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.350
It didn't occur to California law makers

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.270
that many freelancers actually
prefer the flexibility

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.670
that comes with being an
independent contractor.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.870
Companies like Vox, which wrote articles

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:41.118
in support of AB5, cut its
staff of independent contractors

00:01:41.118 --> 00:01:44.280
because it couldn't afford
to bring them on full time.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.460
Labor activists and progressive lawmakers

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.200
were, in effect, deciding
on behalf of workers

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.060
that if they couldn't have a
full-time job with benefits,

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.420
they would be better off
not having a job at all.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.310
For us, this is about focusing on ensuring

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:58.530
that jobs are good jobs, right?

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.730
If we have one or a hundred jobs

00:02:00.730 --> 00:02:02.940
that are paying less than minimum wage,

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.920
there is absolutely no benefit.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.070
We, I rather have, you know, 50 good jobs

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:10.150
than a hundred bad paying jobs.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:13.110
And those 50 good jobs are
actually giving a living wage.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:15.700
Although the law applied
to freelance writers,

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.210
its real targets where
gig economy startups

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.210
like Uber, Lyft and DoorDash.

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.850
Uber and Lyft said that
if AB5 were enforced

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.560
as originally written, it would cost

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.180
hundreds of thousands
of drivers their jobs,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.900
and may even force the companies

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.260
to shut down operations in California.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.320
Basically this is a unions

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.250
versus independent workers struggle.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:40.360
The unions are declining,
except public employees unions,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.630
and so they're desperately
thrashing around

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.510
for people that they could organize.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.615
Williamson Evers is a political scientist

00:02:46.615 --> 00:02:49.480
and a senior fellow at
the Independent Institute,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.230
a libertarian think tank
in Oakland, California.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.370
He says that Uber and
Lyft weren't bluffing

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:56.390
when they said they'd leave California.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:58.950
If you say, well, in California,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.340
it's just to be a sinkhole,
nothing but expense

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.420
and red tape and crazy labor costs

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:08.830
that are not going to be true
in other places, why go there?

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:11.580
If drivers are forced to become employees,

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.060
up to 90% of app based
driving jobs could disappear.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:17.170
The ride sharing companies

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:20.930
spent more than $200
million on proposition 22,

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.870
trying to convince California
voters to exclude them

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:26.230
from AB5, while the law's opponents

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.280
spent roughly $20 million.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:30.750
Proponents of AB five
attribute their defeat

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.040
to the fact that they were outspent.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.530
I don't think money
determines political outcomes.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:38.050
It has an effect on them.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.750
In 2016, Hillary Clinton,
vastly outspent Donald Trump,

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:44.410
and she lost anyway.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:45.807
It's not really determinative.

00:03:45.807 --> 00:03:49.490
The Prop 22 people have
the better argument,

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:53.310
and they took advantage of what they had

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.840
to make their case to the people.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.410
I hope that there's some way that

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.830
the labor movement can look
at freelancers of all types

00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:04.860
and say, this is a valid,
honorable profession,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.830
that we are workers, too.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.090
Many of us choose this field.

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:10.850
We are not exploited.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.300
We're not scabs.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.560
We're small businesses,

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.390
and we deserve to be treated that way.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:20.210
The real exploitation is
the thing that forces people

00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:23.220
to work hours that they
don't want to work.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:25.020
And in ways they don't want to work.

